125
00:14:07,860 --> 00:14:12,220
Avoiding Treaty Pitfalls. Treaties fail in predictable ways.

126
00:14:12,900 --> 00:14:17,380
Here's how this one avoids the common killers: Reservation Games.

127
00:14:18,020 --> 00:14:21,820
Countries love to sign treaties then add exceptions that gut them.

128
00:14:22,240 --> 00:14:25,160
It's like joining a gym but reserving the right to never go.

129
00:14:25,840 --> 00:14:30,400
Solution: No reservations permitted. All or nothing. Take it or leave it.

130
00:14:31,480 --> 00:14:36,920
Ratification Delays. Sign with fanfare, take a photo, then let it die in committee forever.

131
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:42,160
Your species calls this "diplomacy.". Solution: Provisional application.

132
00:14:42,740 --> 00:14:47,930
Takes effect on signature. Ratification just formalizes what already started.

133
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:55,900
Creative Accounting. Reclassify "military" spending as "peacekeeping" or "homeland security" to dodge obligations.

134
00:14:56,280 --> 00:15:03,160
Solution: Incentive Alignment Bonds, or I.A.B.s make creative accounting self-defeating.

135
00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:11,160
Politicians who benefit from treaty compliance have personal financial incentive to ensure their nation's full one percent

136
00:15:11,160 --> 00:15:18,680
contribution flows through. Reclassifying military spending to dodge obligations reduces I.A.B.

137
00:15:18,680 --> 00:15:24,820
returns for that nation's own legislators, the same people who control budget classifications.

138
00:15:26,020 --> 00:15:31,100
Parliamentary Obstacles. Executive signs, legislature refuses to implement.

139
00:15:32,040 --> 00:15:40,890
Solution: The treaty obligates the percentage; Congress implements via the annual National Defense Authorization Act (N.D.A.A.).

140
00:15:41,980 --> 00:15:45,300
After *Medellin v. Texas* (two thousand and eight), the U.S.

141
00:15:45,300 --> 00:15:52,920
Supreme Court made clear that not all treaties are self-executing, and a treaty directing budget appropriations would collide

142
00:15:52,920 --> 00:15:57,720
with Congress's power of the purse. So the treaty doesn't try to bypass Congress.

143
00:15:58,360 --> 00:16:05,900
It creates a binding international obligation that Congress fulfills through its normal appropriations process, the same

144
00:16:05,900 --> 00:16:14,300
way NATO's spending commitments work. The I.A.B. mechanism ensures legislators have a personal financial incentive to include

145
00:16:14,300 --> 00:16:22,580
the one percent line item every year. In other signatory nations, similar provisions route implementation through existing

146
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:32,020
budget processes. Summary. A one percent treaty is a piece of paper that says "Let's spend one percent less on killing and

147
00:16:32,020 --> 00:16:35,960
one percent more on not dying.". That's it. That's the whole thing.

148
00:16:36,480 --> 00:16:41,220
It doesn't end war. It doesn't destroy the military. It doesn't create world peace.

149
00:16:41,600 --> 00:16:46,340
It just moves the *first* one percent of your murder budget to your survival budget.

150
00:16:46,860 --> 00:16:54,540
If you can't agree on that, if you can't agree that spending one penny less on death and one penny more on life is a good

151
00:16:54,540 --> 00:17:01,280
idea, then you deserve whatever's coming. Addendum: The Actual Treaty Text (First Draft).

152
00:17:02,300 --> 00:17:09,800
THE TREATY FOR THE ONE PERCENT REALLOCATION OF MILITARY EXPENDITURES TOWARDS BIOMEDICAL RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT FOR THE

153
00:17:09,800 --> 00:17:18,180
